{"agent_id":"pp3g-zMDt-read-only-analysis-task-for-issue-354-analyze-home","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-10T18:20:14.850380692Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"f2a8a904-548b-469a-bc63-f76f00727bc1","comment_uuid":"9d64ec51-8e93-4f6d-b38b-e1bcfe6403a6","author":"pp3g-zMDt-read-only-analysis-task-for-issue-354-analyze-home","content":"# ANALYSIS: Tools repo monorepo migration — inventory, boundaries, integration points (#354)\n\nREAD-ONLY analysis. No files changed, no moves, no commits. Evidence gathered from filesystem + git-tracked verification of Tools, ASES, tripn-astro, and the crosslink fork (2026-08-10). Ontology per #353: monorepo = EDASES + ASES + Execution layers (separate repos); Tools = general warehouse (NOT a layer, NOT the future Execution Engine); tripn-astro = separate client.\n\n---\n\n## 1. INVENTORY — /home/claude-code/projects/Tools (104 git-tracked files + untracked)\n\n### Tracked content (git ls-files)\n\n| Path | What it is | Who consumes it | Monorepo fit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `README.md` | Repo readme; self-describes as \"a library, not a runnable project\"; catalog pointer to TOOLING.md | All agents via discovery rule | KEEP as warehouse doc — warehouse is sibling to monorepo, not a member |\n| `TOOLING.md` (42.9 KB) | The exhaustive catalog — covers THREE roots: Tools, ASES, user-level (~/.local/bin, ~/.config/opencode, ~/.claude). De facto cross-repo catalog; published as crosslink knowledge page (`crosslink knowledge show tooling`) | All agents in all repos | KEEP — but it is a cross-REPO catalog, not a Tools-only artifact; single source of catalog truth should live in warehouse |\n| `opencode-agent-configuration.md` | Agent/model configuration guide (provider prefixes, dynamic-models plugin, agents table) | OpenCode users/agents | KEEP as warehouse doc |\n| `agents/` (20 .md) | Agent definitions: 16 model-specific subagents (deepseek-flash, gemini-35-flash, gemini-pro-reviewer, glm5.2-auditor, kimi-k2.7-code, laguna-general, ling-general, mimo-*, nemotron-*, north-mini-*, big-pickle) + 4 role agents (auditor/builder/orchestrator/reviewer). Mirrors ~/.config/opencode/agents/ (user-level) + TripN role agents. NOTE: role-agent copies here are STALE vs ASES (auditor/builder/orchestrator/reviewer all differ from ASES .opencode/agents/). | OpenCode (loaded from ~/.config/opencode/agents/ — the user-level copy is the live one; Tools is the mirror) | Warehouse mirror — but the LIVE source is user-level config, not Tools; role-agent authoritative copies live in ASES/tripn .opencode/agents/. Tools is a snapshot, not the source |\n| `scripts/` (3) | CLI wrappers: `claude` (Anthropic→opencode translator), `opencode` (launcher + model-cache refresher), `crosslink-moe` (MoE review orchestrator) | All agents via ~/.local/bin (identical copies deployed) | STALE — see §2.4: ~/.local/bin/claude and ~/.local/bin/opencode are AHEAD of Tools copies (fork identity guard #274, --agent flag, tmux --auto). Tools/scripts/crosslink-moe matches ~/.local/bin. Warehouse should hold the canonical wrapper source; currently the LIVE copies are ahead |\n| `skills/` (2 dirs) | `crosslink-adversarial-review`, `crosslink-subagent-orchestration` — crosslink-orchestration skills. IDENTICAL copies exist in ASES/.claude/skills/ (adversarial-review identical; subagent-orchestration DIFFERS) | Agents in all repos (discovered from .claude/skills/ of the repo they are in) | Warehouse candidate for the shared skills — but duplication already exists between Tools/skills and ASES/.claude/skills |\n| `.claude/skills/` (19 dirs, 22 files tracked) | 18 ASES skills (audit, check, commit, crosslink-guide, design, dev-release, featree, feature, kickoff, maintain, preflight, qa, review-pre-commit, rust-fix-discipline, rust-gpu-discipline, rust-quality, workflow, architect) — mostly IDENTICAL to ASES/.claude/skills/ except kickoff/SKILL.md | Agents in Tools repo itself (and ASES has its own copy) | These are EMBEDDED IN THE CROSSLINK BINARY (see §3) and deployed by `crosslink init`; the Tools copies are git-tracked snapshots of the same content. Warehouse duplication |\n| `.claude/settings.json` | Claude Code hook wiring (work-check, prompt-guard, post-edit-check, pre-web-check, heartbeat, session-start) | Tools repo agents | Warehouse config — but hooks themselves are NOT tracked here |\n| `.crosslink/rules/` (32 files) | 22 language rules (c, cpp, csharp, elixir, go, java, javascript, kotlin, odin, php, python, ruby, rust, scala, shell, swift, typescript, web, zig, +react variants) + cross-cutting (global, quality, rigor, knowledge, project, sanitize-patterns.txt, tooling.md) + 3 tracking modes | All repos: deployed per-repo by `crosslink init` (from crosslink binary embedded resources) and injected by prompt-guard/preflight | Same embedded-resource story as skills — crosslink binary embeds 31 rules; Tools tracks 32 (adds tooling.md). The per-repo copies (ASES) have DRIFTED (global.md, knowledge.md, project.md differ; ASES adds kickoff.md) |\n| `.claude/hooks/` (7 .py, on disk; NOT git-tracked) | work-check.py, prompt-guard.py, post-edit-check.py, pre-web-check.py, heartbeat.py, session-start.py, crosslink_config.py | Tools repo agents (deployed by crosslink init) | NOT TRACKED in Tools — gitignored as auto-generated by crosslink init. Canonical source = crosslink binary embedded resources (§3). On-disk Tools copies are STALE vs the binary current embedded resources (returncode logic differs) |\n| `.claude/commands/` (14, on disk; NOT git-tracked) | Slash-command wrappers mirroring skills | Tools repo agents | NOT TRACKED — deployed by crosslink init from embedded resources |\n| `.claude/mcp/` (3, on disk; NOT git-tracked) | agent-prompt-server.py, knowledge-server.py, safe-fetch-server.py | Tools repo agents (via .mcp.json) | NOT TRACKED — deployed by crosslink init from embedded resources; registered via `.mcp.json` (tracked) |\n| `.mcp.json` | Registers the 3 MCP servers (uv run .claude/mcp/*.py) | Tools repo MCP-capable clients | Warehouse config; the server scripts themselves come from the crosslink binary |\n| `plugins/` (6) | crosslink-guard.ts (32.7 KB), orchestrator-guard.ts (2.3 KB), rtk-guard.ts (13.6 KB) + dynamic-models.ts/plugin.ts/dynamic-models.js (stale stub) | THE GUARD PLUGIN SET — but see §2.1: this Tools copy is the OLDEST of three divergent versions (ASES 41.2 KB, tripn 36.7 KB) | DIVERGED 3 ways — no single source. This is the warehouse most important content and it is currently a stale mirror, not the source |\n| `instructions/` (1) | moe-awareness.md — don not hand-roll multi-model queries, use crosslink-moe | Agents doing MoE reviews | KEEP as warehouse doc |\n| `crosslink/` (1) | moe-config.env (197 B) — MoE orchestrator model config; IDENTICAL to ~/.config/crosslink/moe-config.env. This is NOT the crosslink fork — it is the Tools crosslink/ SUBDIR with a config file | crosslink-moe wrapper | Config artifact; identical copy at user-level — warehouse mirror |\n| `sessions/` (1) | oh-my-opencode-cleanup.md — session cleanup notes | Documentation | Historical note; warehouse doc |\n| `tripn-astro/` (7 files) | PROJECT-SPECIFIC BUNDLE: hooks (generate-status.py, session-start.py, stop-session.py) + scripts (build-combined.mjs, dev-combined.mjs, dev-combined.sh, sync-module.sh) for the tripn-astro client | tripn-astro client repo only | STALE SNAPSHOT — tripn repo has NEWER build-combined.mjs + 3 extra scripts (convert-images, rewrite-image-refs, verify-build-refs); tripn .claude/hooks/session-start.py differs (generic crosslink version vs this tripn-specific orphan-cleanup version). Should NOT go into the monorepo (§5) |\n\n### Untracked / ignored content\n\n| Path | What it is | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.opencode/` (untracked: command/interrupt.md + node_modules + package.json/lock) | OpenCode command dir + bun node_modules | NOT in git — Tools has no .opencode/opencode.json, no .opencode/plugins (guard plugins are NOT consumed from Tools .opencode). OpenCode runtime dir |\n| `.claude/findings-interrupt-plugin.md` (untracked) | Findings plugin doc | Untracked doc |\n| `.crosslink/` runtime state (issues.db, hub-cache, knowledge-cache, keys, agent.json) | Tools own crosslink tracking (it is itself a tracked repo, tracking_mode relaxed) | Machine-local state, gitignored |\n| `.crosslink/.knowledge-cache/server-memory-management.md` | Knowledge cache | Not tracked |\n| Modified-but-uncommitted: `.crosslink/rules/global.md`, `scripts/claude` | Working-tree drift | Tools repo has uncommitted changes — migration must not lose them |\n\n**Inventory summary:** Tools is a MIX of (a) warehouse-unique content (TOOLING.md, scripts/, plugins/, agents/, instructions/, skills/, docs), (b) snapshots of content whose canonical source is the CROSSLINK BINARY (hooks, commands, mcp, skills, rules — deployed by `crosslink init`), and (c) a STALE tripn-astro client bundle. The git-tracked set does NOT include the deployed hooks/commands/mcp — those live on disk but are gitignored.\n\n---\n\n## 2. BOUNDARY ANALYSIS — the dependency graph\n\n### 2.1 Guard plugins — consumed by ASES and tripn; THREE divergent versions\n\n`crosslink-guard.ts` + `orchestrator-guard.ts` + `rtk-guard.ts` are loaded by OpenCode from `.opencode/plugins/` in each repo (referenced in each repo `.opencode/opencode.json` `plugin` array).\n\n| Copy | crosslink-guard.ts size | orchestrator-guard.ts | rtk-guard.ts | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tools/plugins/ (tracked) | 32.7 KB | 2.3 KB | 13.6 KB | Oldest; also holds dynamic-models.ts/plugin.ts (stale vs ~/.config/opencode) |\n| ASES/.opencode/plugins/ (tracked) | 41.2 KB | 3.7 KB | 13.6 KB | Newest (per-role by_type resolution, git-merge gating, chat.params agent resolution — commits gh#109→gh#204) |\n| tripn/.opencode/plugins/ (tracked) | 36.7 KB | 2.3 KB | 13.6 KB | Middle version (synced from ASES with by_type overrides gh#378) |\n| ~/.config/opencode/plugins/ (user-level) | — (has plugin.ts 5.5 KB, dynamic-models.ts 3.2 KB, interrupt.ts) | — | — | User-level dynamic-models with grok/xAI forbidden-model block (#347) — NOT the same file family as guard plugins |\n\n**The plugin duplication is real and diverged 3 ways.** Neither ASES nor tripn consumes Tools/plugins — they maintain their own copies. Tools/plugins is a stale mirror. **This is the sharpest argument for making Tools the single source** — but only if the guard plugins authoritative version moves to Tools AND consumers switch to referencing it (see §4).\n\n### 2.2 Agent definitions — live source is user-level; Tools is a mirror\n\n- `~/.config/opencode/agents/` (16 files) is the LIVE source for the 16 model-specific subagents (Tools/agents mirrors it; mirror note in TOOLING.md says synced 2026-07-31).\n- The 4 role agents (auditor/builder/orchestrator/reviewer) have AUTHORITATIVE copies in ASES/.opencode/agents/ and tripn/.opencode/agents/ (referenced from each repo `.opencode/opencode.json` `agent` section). Tools/agents role copies are STALE (all 4 differ from ASES).\n- tripn-astro role agents deliberately diverge (no git; orchestrator has bounded per-ID removal tools per #349/#350; §5.5 mirror rule keeps ASES and tripn in sync, ASES wins on conflict).\n\n### 2.3 Embedded resources (hooks, commands, mcp, skills, rules) — canonical source is the CROSSLINK BINARY\n\n`crosslink init` deploys, from the crosslink fork `crosslink/resources/` (embedded via build.rs include_str into the binary):\n- `.claude/hooks/*.py` (7 files)\n- `.claude/commands/*.md` (14 files)\n- `.claude/mcp/*.py` (3 files)\n- `.claude/skills/*` (19 skills)\n- `.crosslink/rules/*` (31 rules)\n\n**Each tracked repo (Tools, ASES, tripn, crosslink) gets these deployed.** Tools/ASES `.claude/hooks`, `.claude/commands`, `.claude/mcp` are gitignored (auto-generated) — so the per-repo filesystem copies are NOT source-controlled anywhere; the canonical source is the crosslink fork repo (embedded resources). This means: **for hooks/commands/mcp, Tools is not even a warehouse — it is a deployment artifact location.** The fork repo is the source. Skills and rules ARE git-tracked in Tools/ASES as snapshots, and the per-repo copies have drifted (ASES global.md has background-process + memory-awareness additions; Tools global.md has memory-awareness only; ASES adds kickoff.md rule).\n\n### 2.4 Wrappers (~/.local/bin) — live copies are AHEAD of Tools/scripts\n\n- `~/.local/bin/claude` — AHEAD of Tools/scripts/claude: adds `--agent` flag, CROSSLINK_AGENT_TYPE export, tmux `--auto` auto-approve, updated provider list.\n- `~/.local/bin/opencode` — AHEAD of Tools/scripts/opencode: adds the fork identity drift guard (#274) that refuses to exec unless the resolved binary reports fork identity (marker strings + version check), plus a different REAL path (lib/node_modules/opencode-ai/bin/opencode.exe vs Tools nvm bin path).\n- `~/.local/bin/crosslink-moe` — IDENTICAL to Tools/scripts/crosslink-moe.\n- `~/.cargo/bin/crosslink` — the Rust binary (version 0.9.0-beta.1+a87bd513, built 2026-08-08, one commit behind fork HEAD 37789b51). This is a compiled artifact, NOT source-controlled in Tools.\n\n**The wrapper source-of-truth problem:** Tools/scripts is the \"warehouse copy\" but the LIVE deployed copies are ahead. If Tools is to be the warehouse source, the live copies must be pushed back INTO Tools first (reverse-sync), otherwise migrating Tools-as-source will regress the guard (#274).\n\n### 2.5 Dependency graph (consumption summary)\n\n```\nCROSSLINK FORK REPO (/home/claude-code/projects/crosslink, Rust, 609 tracked)\n  ├─► binary: ~/.cargo/bin/crosslink  (compiled, consumed by ALL)\n  ├─► embedded resources ──(crosslink init)──► .claude/hooks+commands+mcp+skills, .crosslink/rules\n  │        in EVERY tracked repo (Tools, ASES, tripn, crosslink)\n  └─► #349/#350 cleanup work (current feature branch)\n\nASES LAYER REPO\n  ├─► .opencode/plugins/ (guard plugins — NEWEST, self-maintained)\n  ├─► .opencode/agents/ (4 role agents — authoritative for ASES)\n  ├─► .claude/skills/ (deployed + crosslink-adversarial-review + crosslink-subagent-orchestration)\n  ├─► .crosslink/rules/ (deployed + local additions)\n  └─► docs: TOOLING.md knowledge page + tooling.md rule point at Tools\n\ntripn-astro CLIENT REPO (NOT a monorepo member)\n  ├─► .opencode/plugins/ (guard plugins — MIDDLE version, self-maintained)\n  ├─► .opencode/agents/ (4 role agents — diverged: no-git, #349 removal tools)\n  ├─► .claude/hooks/ (deployed + 3 tripn-specific: generate-status, session-start, stop-session)\n  ├─► .claude/skills/ (deployed)\n  └─► its own scripts/ (build-combined etc. — AHEAD of Tools/tripn-astro bundle)\n\nTOOLS WAREHOUSE\n  ├─► TOOLING.md (catalog — consumed by ALL via knowledge page)\n  ├─► scripts/ (stale vs ~/.local/bin)\n  ├─► plugins/ (STALE mirror of guard plugins)\n  ├─► agents/ (STALE mirror of user-level + role agents)\n  ├─► tripn-astro/ (STALE snapshot of tripn client bundle)\n  └─► deployed artifacts of its own crosslink init (hooks/commands/mcp/skills/rules)\n\nUSER-LEVEL (~/.config/opencode, ~/.local/bin, ~/.cargo/bin)\n  ├─► agents/ (LIVE source for 16 subagents)\n  ├─► plugins/ (dynamic-models/plugin/interrupt — LIVE, with #347 grok block)\n  ├─► claude/opencode/crosslink-moe wrappers (LIVE, ahead of Tools for claude+opencode)\n  └─► crosslink binary (compiled from fork)\n```\n\n**Boundary conclusion:** Almost nothing in Tools is a single-source-of-truth TODAY. The only Tools-unique warehouse content that is genuinely authoritative is **TOOLING.md (the catalog)** and the docs. Everything else is either (a) embedded-resource deployment (owned by the crosslink fork), (b) a stale mirror of user-level config, (c) a stale mirror of ASES/tripn plugin+agent copies, or (d) a stale tripn client bundle. **\"Tools = general warehouse\" is the INTENDED ontology (#353), not the current state** — the warehouse role is aspirational; the repo is currently an archive of snapshots.\n\n---\n\n## 3. THE CROSSLINK FORK QUESTION\n\n### What it is\n\n`/home/claude-code/projects/crosslink` is a **separate Rust repository** (609 tracked files; Cargo crate `crosslink` 0.9.0-beta.1; upstream `origin` = https://github.com/dollspace-gay/crosslink, `fork` remote = git@github.com:rock-solid-sites/crosslink). It is a FORK of the upstream crosslink CLI with local modifications (the pp3g fork). The installed binary at ~/.cargo/bin/crosslink is built from this fork.\n\n### Where the #349/#350 work lives\n\nThe cleanup rescope (#349/#350) was implemented IN THE FORK: current branch `feature/pp3g-Zbk6-cleanup-rescope-349`, commit 37789b51 (not pushed). The fork is also where `crosslink init` embedded resources live (`crosslink/resources/` — hooks, skills, commands, mcp, rules — embedded via build.rs and deployed to every tracked repo).\n\n### The Tools `crosslink/` subdir vs the fork\n\n`Tools/crosslink/` contains ONLY `moe-config.env` (197 B — MoE model config, identical to ~/.config/crosslink/moe-config.env). It is a **config file**, not the fork. The fork repo lives at `/home/claude-code/projects/crosslink`, a sibling of Tools under ~/projects/, NOT inside Tools.\n\n### Where should the fork live relative to the monorepo?\n\nAnalysis:\n1. **The fork is NOT a layer.** It is infrastructure tooling — the orchestration CLI that all repos depend on. Per #353 it belongs in the warehouse category (\"forks (crosslink), plugins (guard plugins, dynamic-models), wrappers (claude, opencode, rtk, crosslink-moe)\").\n2. **But the fork is a full software project** (Rust source, tests, docs, vscode-extension, dashboard, justfile) with its own upstream/remote — it cannot be \"absorbed\" into a monorepo as a subdirectory without losing its fork identity (upstream sync, its own issue tracker, cargo publishing).\n3. **The fork is the SOURCE of the embedded resources** that every repo consumes at init time. This gives it a unique position: it is the most-consumed Tools-category artifact.\n4. **Practical options:**\n   - **(A) Fork stays a sibling repo** (~/projects/crosslink), cataloged in TOOLING.md §16 (already is), binary installed to ~/.cargo/bin. The monorepo repos consume it via the installed binary + `crosslink init`. **This matches current reality and preserves fork identity.** Tools documents it; it is not INSIDE Tools.\n   - **(B) Fork moves inside Tools** (e.g. Tools/crosslink-fork/) — would make the warehouse self-contained but breaks the fork own git/remote/upstream workflow and mixes a Rust project with the warehouse. NOT recommended without operator direction.\n   - **(C) Fork becomes a 4th monorepo member** — contradicts #353 ontology (monorepo = 3 LAYER repos; Tools is the side warehouse). NOT recommended.\n5. **Recommendation:** (A) — the fork stays a sibling repo, tracked in the warehouse CATALOG (TOOLING.md), with its embedded resources recognized as the canonical source for init-deployed content. The warehouse does NOT need to hold copies of hooks/skills/commands/mcp/rules that the binary embeds — that is duplication with drift (proven: global.md, kickoff/SKILL.md, work-check.py all differ between Tools snapshot and fork embedded resources).\n\n---\n\n## 4. INTEGRATION POINTS — how the monorepo would consume Tools\n\nGiven Tools-is-a-warehouse (a side repo, not a layer member), the monorepo three layer repos need to REFERENCE Tools content. Options:\n\n### Option A: Git submodule\n- **Mechanism:** monorepo adds `tools/` submodule → points at Tools repo (or a monorepo-org fork of it).\n- **Pros:** versioned pointer; no content copy in each layer repo; one update point.\n- **Cons:** submodules are painful for agents (need init/recursive checkout; crosslink worktrees + submodules = extra friction); the consumers that matter (hooks/skills/rules) are deployed by the binary, not read from a submodule; guard plugins/agents are loaded from each repo `.opencode/` by OpenCode — OpenCode does not load plugins from a submodule path by default.\n- **Fit:** Moderate. Best for the WAREHOUSE DOCS + scripts + plugins-as-source IF a sync mechanism is added.\n\n### Option B: Separate package (npm/pip/crates)\n- **Mechanism:** package the guard plugins (npm package), the skills, the rules; consumers install.\n- **Pros:** clean dependency semantics; versioning; the plugins already have package.json/node_modules scaffolding (Tools/.opencode, ASES/.opencode both have bun/node module dirs).\n- **Cons:** the content is mostly markdown/python/bash/ts configs that OpenCode/Claude Code discover by PATH, not by package; rules/skills are PATH-based discovery; a package would require a fetch/install step on every worktree — heavier than symlink.\n- **Fit:** Low-medium. Over-engineered for a warehouse.\n\n### Option C: Vendored copy (status quo)\n- **Mechanism:** each layer repo copies the content it needs (current state).\n- **Pros:** nothing new to build; repos are self-contained.\n- **Cons:** PROVEN divergence — the guard plugins are already 3 versions apart; rules/skills drift; nothing enforces sync. This is the failure mode the monorepo migration should fix, not preserve.\n\n### Option D: Symlink to a shared location\n- **Mechanism:** each repo `.opencode/plugins/` etc. become symlinks into the Tools repo (or a shared ~/.config-like location).\n- **Pros:** single source of truth; live updates; zero copy.\n- **Cons:** symlinks are machine-absolute (breaks other machines/CI — bad for a monorepo meant to be portable); git tracks symlinks as pointers but crosslink worktrees copy them; the guard plugins reference repo-relative state; the deployed hooks/commands/mcp are deployed BY the binary, so symlinking them conflicts with `crosslink init` regeneration.\n- **Fit:** Low for cross-machine portability; workable only for single-machine server setups.\n\n### Option E (RECOMMENDED shape): Tools stays a sibling warehouse + explicit SYNC SCRIPT from Tools into consumers\n- **Mechanism:** Tools holds the canonical source for warehouse-unique content (guard plugins, wrappers, agent defs, skills, rules, docs). A `sync-tooling.sh` in Tools (or per-consumer) copies the authoritative files into each layer repo `.opencode/plugins/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.crosslink/rules/` — like `crosslink init` does for embedded resources but for warehouse content. Consumers commit the synced copies (self-contained, portable), and the sync script + a checksum/version marker make drift detectable.\n- **Pros:** keeps repos portable (vendored copies) while making Tools the real source (no manual copy drift); mirrors the existing `crosslink init` deployment pattern; works with OpenCode/Claude Code PATH-based discovery; version-checkable.\n- **Cons:** adds a sync step to the workflow; requires the \"which version is authoritative\" decision (see §5 step 3).\n- **Note on .opencode/plugins duplication (ASES vs tripn):** making Tools the single source + a sync script is exactly the fix for the 3-version divergence — BUT it only works if (1) the authoritative (newest ASES) version is pushed into Tools FIRST, and (2) the sync script becomes part of the workflow (or a guard that fails on version mismatch, like the opencode fork identity guard #274). tripn-astro, as a client, would reference Tools for the guard plugins instead of maintaining its own copy — per #353, tripn consumes via its `.ases/` boundary, so the sync path for tripn is the boundary, not direct repo access.\n\n**Integration conclusion:** submodule/package are overkill for PATH-discovered content; vendored-copy is the proven-failure status quo; symlink breaks portability. The pragmatic recommendation is **Tools stays a sibling warehouse, canonical versions pushed back into Tools, and an explicit sync/deploy script distributes warehouse content into layer repos (and the tripn client via its .ases boundary)** — mirroring how the crosslink binary already distributes embedded resources.\n\n---\n\n## 5. MIGRATION PLAN (ordered steps, risks, what must NOT move)\n\n### What must NOT move (hard constraints)\n\n1. **tripn-astro bundle** (`Tools/tripn-astro/`) — client-specific, STALE snapshot; the tripn repo is the live source. Do NOT move into the monorepo; either delete from Tools or leave as historical archive. Per #353 tripn is not a monorepo member.\n2. **User-level config** (`~/.config/opencode/`, `~/.claude/`, `~/.local/bin/`, `~/.cargo/bin/`) — the LIVE agent config, wrappers, and crosslink binary are outside any repo and must not be relocated. Migration must NOT touch these paths.\n3. **Live binary paths** — `~/.cargo/bin/crosslink`, `~/.local/bin/claude|opencode|crosslink-moe|rtk`, `~/.local/bin` utilities. These are deployment artifacts; the SOURCE (wrappers in Tools/scripts) may be normalized, but the deployed paths stay.\n4. **The crosslink fork repo** (`/home/claude-code/projects/crosslink`) — stays a sibling repo with its own remotes/upstream (§3). Its embedded resources are the canonical source for init-deployed content; do not duplicate them into the monorepo.\n5. **Machine-local state** — Tools own `.crosslink/` runtime (issues.db, hub-cache, keys), node_modules, `.opencode/` runtime dir.\n\n### Ordered steps\n\n1. **Reverse-sync live content into Tools (pre-migration normalization).**\n   - Push ~/.local/bin/claude and ~/.local/bin/opencode back into Tools/scripts (the #274 fork guard and --agent logic must become the Tools canonical versions, not be lost).\n   - Resolve the guard-plugin divergence: designate ONE authoritative version (the ASES newest is the sensible default) and update Tools/plugins to it; document the decision on the issue (#354 deliverable flags it; the actual push is a follow-up implementation task).\n   - Sync the 2 crosslink skills (Tools/skills vs ASES/.claude/skills) and role-agent defs so Tools is a true mirror, OR explicitly mark them as mirrors of ASES/tripn (not the source) in TOOLING.md.\n   - Commit the uncommitted Tools changes (.crosslink/rules/global.md, scripts/claude) before any restructuring.\n2. **Decide Tools canonical scope** — operator decision on which of these Tools owns as source vs mirrors:\n   - OWN: TOOLING.md (catalog), scripts/ (wrappers), plugins/ (guard plugins), skills/ (the 2 crosslink skills), agents/ (mirror of user-level — or documented as mirror), docs, instructions/, crosslink/moe-config.env (or documented as mirror of ~/.config/crosslink).\n   - MIRROR (canonical elsewhere): hooks/commands/mcp (crosslink binary), .claude/skills + rules (crosslink binary, but ASES has local overrides), role agents (ASES/tripn), tripn-astro bundle (tripn repo).\n3. **Build the sync mechanism** (implementation follow-up): `sync-tooling.sh` in Tools that copies authoritative warehouse content into each layer repo `.opencode/plugins/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.crosslink/rules/`, and to tripn via its `.ases/` boundary; include a version/checksum marker so drift is detectable (mirror the #274 fork-identity-guard pattern — fail loudly on mismatch).\n4. **Form the monorepo** (separate initiative): the three layer repos (EDASES/ASES/Execution) each remain separate repos in the monorepo; Tools remains a sibling warehouse. No Tools content is absorbed INTO the monorepo; the monorepo references Tools via the sync mechanism + TOOLING.md catalog.\n5. **Update TOOLING.md** to reflect the resolved source-of-truth map (what Tools owns, what it mirrors, where the canonical copy lives) and the sync procedure — so the catalog matches the ontology instead of the current snapshot reality.\n6. **Migrate the catalog knowledge page** — ensure `crosslink knowledge show tooling` points at the monorepo-era catalog (may move with Tools repo or stay as a knowledge page).\n\n### Risks\n\n- **Regression of live wrappers** if Tools/scripts is treated as canonical without the reverse-sync (#274 fork guard, --agent, tmux --auto would be lost).\n- **Guard-plugin regression** if the sync picks the wrong authoritative version (3 divergent copies exist; ASES has the newest, tripn is mid, Tools is oldest).\n- **Crosslink binary staleness** — installed binary (a87bd513) is one commit behind fork HEAD (37789b51); the embedded-resource deploy will change when the binary is rebuilt. Any snapshot taken of Tools/.claude/* must not be treated as canonical.\n- **§5.5 two-repo sync (ASES↔tripn) interplay** — role agents and playbook must stay in sync between ASES and tripn regardless of warehouse changes; a Tools-based sync must not break the ASES-wins-on-conflict rule.\n- **Lock/issue state loss** — Tools has its own crosslink issue DB (issues #103/#105 etc.); migrating the repo must not lose its tracked history (it is a git repo with its own remote rss-tools).\n\n---\n\n## 6. WHAT-NOT-TESTED / OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n### What-not-tested (negative space)\n\n- **I did not run `crosslink init` in any repo** — the claim that hooks/commands/mcp/skills/rules are deployed from the binary embedded resources is based on reading `crosslink/crosslink/src/commands/init/mod.rs` (include_str!/SKILL_FILES/RULES deployment code) + the .gitignore markers + diff evidence (fork embedded vs on-disk copies differ where the binary was rebuilt after the on-disk deploy). The exact byte-for-byte deploy behavior on a fresh repo is NOT executed-tested.\n- **I did not diff EVERY file** between Tools/ASES/tripn — I diffed the guard plugins (all 3 versions), the 4 role agents, the hooks (work-check.py), skills (kickoff), rules (global.md/knowledge.md/project.md), and the wrappers. Other files may also drift.\n- **I did not verify the fork embedded resources are current with the installed binary** — the installed binary is a87bd513; the fork repo HEAD is 37789b51 (newer). The on-disk Tools/ASES hooks appear to match an OLDER embedded version (returncode==2 logic) than the fork current resources (returncode!=2 fail-open logic) — consistent with the binary being rebuilt after the last `crosslink init`, but not verified end-to-end.\n- **I did not test whether OpenCode can load guard plugins from a submodule/symlink path** — the integration-point tradeoffs in §4 assume PATH/relative-./plugins/ loading; plugin loading from a submodule is untested.\n- **I did not inventory every file in ASES/tripn** beyond the Tools-consumption boundary (the task scope); TOOLING.md §7.2/§9 lists ASES-side content but I did not diff all of it.\n\n### Open questions for the operator\n\n1. **Guard-plugin authority:** which version of crosslink-guard.ts/orchestrator-guard.ts is authoritative (ASES 41.2 KB is newest — confirm)? Should Tools/plugins become the single source, and should ASES/tripn switch to a sync-based copy?\n2. **Wrapper canonicalization:** is the operator OK pushing ~/.local/bin/claude + opencode back into Tools/scripts as the canonical source (reverse-sync), or should the wrappers remain user-level-only with Tools as a mirror?\n3. **Warehouse scope:** which of the \"mirror\" categories does Tools continue to track as snapshots (skills/rules/agents), and which should be documented as mirrors only? Should the embedded-resource snapshots (hooks/commands/mcp) be REMOVED from the Tools filesystem (they are gitignored anyway) to avoid stale-artifact confusion?\n4. **Sync mechanism:** does the operator want a `sync-tooling.sh` deploy script (recommended), or a package/submodule approach? This decides step 3 of the migration plan.\n5. **tripn-astro bundle:** delete from Tools, or keep as historical archive? (Not a monorepo member either way.)\n6. **Fork placement:** confirm the fork stays a sibling repo under ~/projects/crosslink (recommended) rather than moving inside Tools or joining the monorepo.\n7. **Monorepo repo layout:** the monorepo is described as EDASES+ASES+Execution as separate repos — is the monorepo a git monorepo (one repo, three directories) or a repo-of-repos (submodules)? This changes whether \"Tools is a sibling\" means sibling-directory or sibling-repo. #353 says \"separate repos\" — confirm the container mechanism.","created_at":"2026-08-10T18:20:14.843684073Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:8xW0Zo/6aknDReD/uWECt7Bz5Z9+z/4hZhKxDGc5BhA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg3pY2Mbp0xlKax8jIMF3lZT2x6aC0Jm0nfKp+FzPFaK8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABArpV/WsiZBK6zCaprgkjFwnZ63OBMC/Y1TT8r78eb22fcbfe1wvjYO8FandGTVmkkhTpBSAmxjhC6c13YA9KkAA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:8xW0Zo/6aknDReD/uWECt7Bz5Z9+z/4hZhKxDGc5BhA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg3pY2Mbp0xlKax8jIMF3lZT2x6aC0Jm0nfKp+FzPFaK8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQLcjz1lnH/YM8LXwjPtfxYllvzxvrWDnLm8V6SBW0tXGH/HdD5ehds45uKMS9UfZqP50BRKcFs8GtMHe0jFGawo="}
